"Yes, an order with SPRING15 would fail the same way SUMMER25 did. I recorded this with ReportInvestigation for this run, and I changed no code.\n\n- **Code path:** \u0060GetOrderTotal\u0060 returns early only when the code is empty. Otherwise it runs \u0060DiscountRates[order.DiscountCode]\u0060 at line 18. SPRING15 is not a key in that table, so it throws \u0060KeyNotFoundException\u0060, just as SUMMER25 does.\n- **Data:** \u0060data/orders.csv\u0060 has no order with SPRING15. The codes in the file are WELCOME10, VIP20, SUMMER25 (order 1003), FREESHIP (order 1011), and blank. A search of the whole order-service folder for SPRING15 found nothing. So the sample data does not contain the case, but a live order could still carry it.\n- **Proposed fix:** Yes, the TryGetValue with a 0 rate fallback covers SPRING15 with no other change. It does not look at the specific code, so any retired or unknown code gets no discount. Valid codes keep their rates, so totals for them do not change.\n\nOne thing the fix does not touch: \u0060GetDiscountLabel\u0060 in \u0060Api/DiscountLabel.cs\u0060 does not use the table. For SPRING15 it would show \u0022SPRING (15% off)\u0022 on the receipt, even though no discount is applied. That does not throw, but the label would be misleading.\n\n[Answered by triage.investigator, did: SearchWorkspace: No matches found.; ReadWorkspaceFile: id,customer,items,discount_code,payment_ref,refunded,created,country,gift_messa\u2026; ReportInvestigation: Recorded step 5 of run eval-order-total-1003-again-20261127-060003-triage-1: Th\u2026]"